11
00:00:50.482 --> 00:00:57.295
Primitive assembly groups vertices into
points, lines, or triangles.

12
00:00:57.295 --> 00:01:05.096
Consider this worked case: A polygon with one
vertex outside the camera frustum is clipped

13
00:01:05.096 --> 00:01:08.217
to intersect the view volume boundaries.

14
00:01:08.217 --> 00:01:14.656
Separate the formal definition from an
implementation detail, then identify what

15
00:01:14.656 --> 00:01:16.998
evidence would distinguish them.

16
00:01:16.998 --> 00:01:23.638
A precise definition makes later examples
easier to evaluate and revise.

17
00:01:23.638 --> 00:01:30.730
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

18
00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:32.857
throughout the analysis.

19
00:01:32.978 --> 00:01:40.924
With the foundation in place, examine how
Rasterization and Interpolation operates in a

20
00:01:40.924 --> 00:01:42.146
concrete sequence.

21
00:01:42.146 --> 00:01:47.771
Interpolation produces per-fragment values
from vertex outputs.

22
00:01:47.771 --> 00:01:56.010
Consider this worked case: A triangle is
rasterized into a set of pixels, with color

23
00:01:56.010 --> 00:02:02.052
and normal values interpolated across each
pixel based on barycentric coordinates.

24
00:02:02.053 --> 00:02:09.427
Follow every arrow in order and name the state
before and after each transition.

25
00:02:09.427 --> 00:02:15.084
The trace converts an abstract statement into
an inspectable sequence.

26
00:02:15.084 --> 00:02:22.596
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

27
00:02:22.596 --> 00:02:24.849
throughout the analysis.

28
00:02:24.962 --> 00:02:32.500
Next, compare the choices involved in Fragment
Processing and Depth Testing and make the

29
00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:34.115
decision criteria explicit.

30
00:02:34.115 --> 00:02:39.755
Depth testing resolves visibility according to
stored depth values.

31
00:02:39.755 --> 00:02:45.925
Consider this worked case: A backface-occluded
triangle is discarded because its depth value

32
00:02:45.925 --> 00:02:51.146
is greater than the stored depth buffer value
at that pixel.

33
00:02:51.146 --> 00:02:58.325
Compare alternatives against the same
constraints so the evaluation does not move

34
00:02:58.325 --> 00:02:59.521
the goalposts.

35
00:02:59.521 --> 00:03:06.630
Using shared criteria turns a preference into
a defensible technical choice.

36
00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:13.938
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

37
00:03:13.938 --> 00:03:16.130
throughout the analysis.

38
00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:24.533
Apply Blending and Coordinate Spaces to a
worked situation instead of treating it as an

39
00:03:24.533 --> 00:03:25.637
isolated definition.

40
00:03:25.637 --> 00:03:32.246
Coordinate spaces, sampling, and numerical
precision must remain explicit.

41
00:03:32.246 --> 00:03:39.863
Consider this worked case: A semi-transparent
overlay is blended with the underlying scene

42
00:03:39.863 --> 00:03:45.137
using alpha blending to produce a final pixel
color.

43
00:03:45.137 --> 00:03:52.374
Work from the concrete inputs to the result,
checking units, types, or invariants at each

44
00:03:52.374 --> 00:03:52.856
step.

45
00:03:52.856 --> 00:03:57.434
The worked case shows both how to apply the
method and how to check it.

46
00:03:57.554 --> 00:04:04.212
Examine the tradeoffs in Geometric Modeling
and Transformations, including what improves

47
00:04:04.212 --> 00:04:07.238
and what becomes more difficult.

48
00:04:07.238 --> 00:04:13.785
Computer graphics uses geometric models to
represent objects and scenes.

49
00:04:13.785 --> 00:04:21.281
Consider this worked case: A 3-D model is
translated, rotated, and scaled using

50
00:04:21.281 --> 00:04:25.894
transformation matrices to position it in the
scene.

51
00:04:25.894 --> 00:04:32.457
State the benefit, the cost, and the condition
under which the tradeoff is acceptable.

52
00:04:32.457 --> 00:04:39.582
Making the tradeoff visible prevents a local
improvement from hiding a larger cost.

53
00:04:39.582 --> 00:04:46.745
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

54
00:04:46.745 --> 00:04:48.894
throughout the analysis.

55
00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:56.185
Stress-test the reasoning around Viewing and
Projection with an edge case and identify

56
00:04:56.185 --> 00:04:58.944
which assumption controls the outcome.

57
00:04:58.944 --> 00:05:03.522
Viewing maps a scene through a camera model to
an image.

58
00:05:03.522 --> 00:05:10.424
Consider this worked case: A perspective
projection matrix converts 3-D world

59
00:05:10.424 --> 00:05:15.444
coordinates into 2-D screen coordinates,
simulating camera depth.

60
00:05:15.444 --> 00:05:22.381
Change one assumption at a time and observe
which part of the reasoning no longer holds.

61
00:05:22.381 --> 00:05:29.741
The edge case reveals the boundary of the
claim without discarding the useful core idea.

62
00:05:29.858 --> 00:05:37.415
Use Shading and Texture Mapping to diagnose a
failure by separating symptoms, causes, and

63
00:05:37.415 --> 00:05:38.495
corrective action.

64
00:05:38.495 --> 00:05:43.870
Texture mapping associates image data with
geometric surfaces.

65
00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:50.025
Consider this worked case: A diffuse shading
model computes surface brightness based on

66
00:05:50.025 --> 00:05:57.600
light direction and surface normal, while a
texture map applies a 2-D image to a 3-D

67
00:05:57.600 --> 00:05:58.073
surface.

68
00:05:58.073 --> 00:06:05.704
Locate the first point where observed behavior
diverges from expected behavior, then test the

69
00:06:05.704 --> 00:06:07.339
smallest plausible cause.

70
00:06:07.339 --> 00:06:15.432
The diagnosis is strongest when another person
can reproduce the observation and the test.

71
00:06:15.554 --> 00:06:22.816
Connect Hardware Shaders and Rasterization
Focus to the earlier ideas and explain how the

72
00:06:22.816 --> 00:06:25.410
pieces work as one system.

73
00:06:25.410 --> 00:06:31.679
The course will focus on rasterization
techniques and emphasize the hardware

74
00:06:31.679 --> 00:06:36.238
rasterization pipeline including the use of
hardware shaders.

75
00:06:36.238 --> 00:06:44.571
Consider this worked case: A fragment shader
computes lighting and color values for each

76
00:06:44.571 --> 00:06:49.332
pixel in parallel on the GPU, accelerating
rasterization.

77
00:06:49.332 --> 00:06:58.975
Explain each connection explicitly and verify
that information retains the same meaning

78
00:06:58.975 --> 00:07:00.582
across representations.

79
00:07:00.582 --> 00:07:07.816
The synthesis matters only if every connection
remains supported by the original evidence.

80
00:07:07.922 --> 00:07:14.568
Finish with Practical Analysis Checklist as a
practical checklist that can guide a new

81
00:07:14.568 --> 00:07:15.043
analysis.

82
00:07:15.043 --> 00:07:22.005
Visual correctness follows from explicit
conventions, assumptions, sampling decisions,

83
00:07:22.005 --> 00:07:24.325
and measurable error.

84
00:07:24.325 --> 00:07:31.414
Consider this worked case: A renderer checks
anti-aliasing, depth precision, and sampling

85
00:07:31.414 --> 00:07:36.731
rates to ensure measurable error remains
within acceptable bounds.

86
00:07:36.731 --> 00:07:44.693
For each checklist item, record the evidence,
the remaining uncertainty, and the next

87
00:07:44.693 --> 00:07:45.918
verification step.

88
00:07:45.918 --> 00:07:52.729
The result is a repeatable procedure, not a
conclusion that depends on memory or

89
00:07:52.729 --> 00:07:53.215
